<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-trigonometric-identities">
<title>Trigonometric Identities</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Angles, radians, right-triangle trigonometry, the unit circle,
algebraic manipulation, inverse trigonometric functions,
and basic complex-number notation.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this reference section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>identify the six fundamental trigonometric functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use reciprocal identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use quotient identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use Pythagorean identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize even and odd trigonometric functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use periodicity identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply cofunction identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use reference-angle relationships;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use sum and difference identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use double-angle identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use half-angle identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use power-reduction identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use product-to-sum identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use sum-to-product identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize multiple-angle identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>express trigonometric functions using complex exponentials;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use inverse-trigonometric relationships;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>identify principal-value ranges of inverse trigonometric functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>simplify compositions involving trig and inverse trig functions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>derive selected identities from the unit circle or Euler's formula;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>solve identity-verification problems carefully;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize common false trigonometric identities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>track domain restrictions when dividing by trigonometric quantities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>connect trigonometric identities with calculus, differential equations,
          Fourier analysis, complex analysis, geometry, and applied mathematics.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-six-trig-functions-reference">
<title>The Six Trigonometric Functions</title>
<p>For an angle <m>\theta</m>, the six basic trigonometric functions are</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\theta,
\quad
\cos\theta,
\quad
\tan\theta,
\quad
\csc\theta,
\quad
\sec\theta,
\quad
\cot\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>On the unit circle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\theta=x,
\qquad
\sin\theta=y.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus a point on the unit circle has coordinates</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(\cos\theta,\sin\theta).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-right-triangle-trig-definitions">
<title>Right-Triangle Definitions</title>
<p>For an acute angle <m>\theta</m> in a right triangle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\theta
=
\frac{
\text{opposite}
}{
\text{hypotenuse}
},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\theta
=
\frac{
\text{adjacent}
}{
\text{hypotenuse}
},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan\theta
=
\frac{
\text{opposite}
}{
\text{adjacent}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-reciprocal-trig-identities">
<title>Reciprocal Identities</title>
<p>The reciprocal identities are</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc\theta
=
\frac{1}{\sin\theta},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec\theta
=
\frac{1}{\cos\theta},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot\theta
=
\frac{1}{\tan\theta}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\theta
=
\frac{1}{\csc\theta},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\theta
=
\frac{1}{\sec\theta},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan\theta
=
\frac{1}{\cot\theta}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quotient-trig-identities">
<title>Quotient Identities</title>
<p>The quotient identities are</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan\theta
=
\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot\theta
=
\frac{\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These are valid wherever the denominators are nonzero.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-primary-pythagorean-trig">
<title>Primary Pythagorean Identity</title>
<p>From the unit circle equation</p>

<p>
<md>x^2+y^2=1,</md>
</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>x=\cos\theta,
\qquad
y=\sin\theta,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-secondary-pythagorean-trig">
<title>Secondary Pythagorean Identities</title>
<p>Divide</p>

<p>
<md>\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1</md>
</p>

<p>by <m>\cos^2\theta</m>, where <m>\cos\theta\neq0</m>:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan^2\theta+1
=
\sec^2\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Divide by <m>\sin^2\theta</m>, where <m>\sin\theta\neq0</m>:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1+\cot^2\theta
=
\csc^2\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-pythagorean-trig-rearrangements">
<title>Pythagorean Rearrangements</title>
<p>Useful equivalent forms include</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1-\sin^2\theta
=
\cos^2\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1-\cos^2\theta
=
\sin^2\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec^2\theta-\tan^2\theta
=
1,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc^2\theta-\cot^2\theta
=
1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-even-odd-trig-identities">
<title>Even and Odd Identities</title>
<p>Cosine and secant are even:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(-\theta)
=
\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec(-\theta)
=
\sec\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Sine, tangent, cosecant, and cotangent are odd:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(-\theta)
=
-\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(-\theta)
=
-\tan\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc(-\theta)
=
-\csc\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot(-\theta)
=
-\cot\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-trig-periodicity-reference">
<title>Periodicity</title>
<p>Sine and cosine have period <m>2\pi</m>:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\theta+2\pi)
=
\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\theta+2\pi)
=
\cos\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec(\theta+2\pi)
=
\sec\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc(\theta+2\pi)
=
\csc\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-cotangent-period">
<title>Tangent and Cotangent Periodicity</title>
<p>Tangent and cotangent have period <m>\pi</m>:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\theta+\pi)
=
\tan\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot(\theta+\pi)
=
\cot\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-cofunction-identities-reference">
<title>Cofunction Identities</title>
<p>For complementary angles,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\left(
\frac{\pi}{2}-\theta
\right)
=
\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\left(
\frac{\pi}{2}-\theta
\right)
=
\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan\left(
\frac{\pi}{2}-\theta
\right)
=
\cot\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot\left(
\frac{\pi}{2}-\theta
\right)
=
\tan\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec\left(
\frac{\pi}{2}-\theta
\right)
=
\csc\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc\left(
\frac{\pi}{2}-\theta
\right)
=
\sec\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-reference-angle-sign-patterns">
<title>Reference-Angle Sign Patterns</title>
<p>The signs of sine and cosine determine the signs of all six trigonometric
functions.</p>

<p>A common quadrant mnemonic is:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{Quadrant} &amp; \text{Positive Functions} \\ \hline
I &amp; \text{all} \\
II &amp; \sin,\csc \\
III &amp; \tan,\cot \\
IV &amp; \cos,\sec
\end{array}
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quadrant-reduction-identities">
<title>Quadrant Reduction Identities</title>
<p>Useful identities include</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\pi-\theta)
=
\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\pi-\theta)
=
-\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\pi-\theta)
=
-\tan\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\pi+\theta)
=
-\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\pi+\theta)
=
-\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\pi+\theta)
=
\tan\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-additional-reduction-identities">
<title>Additional Reduction Identities</title>
<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(2\pi-\theta)
=
-\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(2\pi-\theta)
=
\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(2\pi-\theta)
=
-\tan\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-sum-identity">
<title>Sine Sum Identity</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\alpha+\beta)
=
\sin\alpha\cos\beta
+
\cos\alpha\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-difference-identity">
<title>Sine Difference Identity</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\alpha-\beta)
=
\sin\alpha\cos\beta
-
\cos\alpha\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-cosine-sum-identity">
<title>Cosine Sum Identity</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha+\beta)
=
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
-
\sin\alpha\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-cosine-difference-identity">
<title>Cosine Difference Identity</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha-\beta)
=
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
+
\sin\alpha\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-sum-identity">
<title>Tangent Sum Identity</title>
<p>Where defined,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\alpha+\beta)
=
\frac{
\tan\alpha+\tan\beta
}{
1-\tan\alpha\tan\beta
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-difference-identity">
<title>Tangent Difference Identity</title>
<p>Where defined,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\alpha-\beta)
=
\frac{
\tan\alpha-\tan\beta
}{
1+\tan\alpha\tan\beta
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-sine-sum">
<title>Worked Example: Exact Sine Value</title>
<example>
<title>Find <m>\sin75^\circ</m></title>
<p>Write</p>

<p>
<md>75^\circ
=
45^\circ+30^\circ.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\sin75^\circ
=
\sin45^\circ\cos30^\circ
+
\cos45^\circ\sin30^\circ.</md>
</p>

<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>\sin45^\circ
=
\cos45^\circ
=
\frac{\sqrt2}{2},</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\cos30^\circ
=
\frac{\sqrt3}{2},</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\sin30^\circ
=
\frac12,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\sin75^\circ
=
\frac{\sqrt6}{4}
+
\frac{\sqrt2}{4}.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin75^\circ
=
\frac{
\sqrt6+\sqrt2
}{4}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-cosine-difference">
<title>Worked Example: Exact Cosine Value</title>
<example>
<title>Find <m>\cos15^\circ</m></title>
<p>Write</p>

<p>
<md>15^\circ
=
45^\circ-30^\circ.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\cos15^\circ
=
\cos45^\circ\cos30^\circ
+
\sin45^\circ\sin30^\circ.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos15^\circ
=
\frac{
\sqrt6+\sqrt2
}{4}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-double-angle">
<title>Double-Angle Identity for Sine</title>
<p>Set</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha=\beta=\theta</md>
</p>

<p>in the sine sum identity:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin2\theta
=
2\sin\theta\cos\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-cosine-double-angle">
<title>Double-Angle Identity for Cosine</title>
<p>From the cosine sum identity,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos2\theta
=
\cos^2\theta-\sin^2\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>\sin^2\theta+\cos^2\theta=1,</md>
</p>

<p>we also obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos2\theta
=
2\cos^2\theta-1,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos2\theta
=
1-2\sin^2\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-double-angle">
<title>Double-Angle Identity for Tangent</title>
<p>Where defined,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan2\theta
=
\frac{
2\tan\theta
}{
1-\tan^2\theta
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-power-reduction">
<title>Power-Reduction Identity for Sine</title>
<p>From</p>

<p>
<md>\cos2\theta
=
1-2\sin^2\theta,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^2\theta
=
\frac{
1-\cos2\theta
}{2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-cosine-power-reduction">
<title>Power-Reduction Identity for Cosine</title>
<p>From</p>

<p>
<md>\cos2\theta
=
2\cos^2\theta-1,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos^2\theta
=
\frac{
1+\cos2\theta
}{2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-half-angle">
<title>Half-Angle Identity for Sine</title>
<p>Replacing <m>\theta</m> by <m>\theta/2</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^2
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right)
=
\frac{
1-\cos\theta
}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right)
=
\pm
\sqrt{
\frac{
1-\cos\theta
}{2}
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The sign depends on the quadrant of <m>\theta/2</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-cosine-half-angle">
<title>Half-Angle Identity for Cosine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos^2
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right)
=
\frac{
1+\cos\theta
}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right)
=
\pm
\sqrt{
\frac{
1+\cos\theta
}{2}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-half-angle">
<title>Half-Angle Identity for Tangent</title>
<p>Useful forms include</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right)
=
\frac{
\sin\theta
}{
1+\cos\theta
},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right)
=
\frac{
1-\cos\theta
}{
\sin\theta
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Where square-root form is appropriate,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right)
=
\pm
\sqrt{
\frac{
1-\cos\theta
}{
1+\cos\theta
}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-triple-angle">
<title>Triple-Angle Identity for Sine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin3\theta
=
3\sin\theta
-
4\sin^3\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-cosine-triple-angle">
<title>Triple-Angle Identity for Cosine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos3\theta
=
4\cos^3\theta
-
3\cos\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-triple-angle">
<title>Triple-Angle Identity for Tangent</title>
<p>Where defined,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan3\theta
=
\frac{
3\tan\theta-\tan^3\theta
}{
1-3\tan^2\theta
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-product-to-sum-sine-sine">
<title>Product-to-Sum: Sine-Sine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\alpha\sin\beta
=
\frac12
\left[
\cos(\alpha-\beta)
-
\cos(\alpha+\beta)
\right].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-product-to-sum-cos-cos">
<title>Product-to-Sum: Cosine-Cosine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
=
\frac12
\left[
\cos(\alpha-\beta)
+
\cos(\alpha+\beta)
\right].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-product-to-sum-sine-cosine">
<title>Product-to-Sum: Sine-Cosine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\alpha\cos\beta
=
\frac12
\left[
\sin(\alpha+\beta)
+
\sin(\alpha-\beta)
\right].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-product-to-sum-cosine-sine">
<title>Product-to-Sum: Cosine-Sine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\alpha\sin\beta
=
\frac12
\left[
\sin(\alpha+\beta)
-
\sin(\alpha-\beta)
\right].
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sum-to-product-sine-sum">
<title>Sum-to-Product: Sine Sum</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\alpha+\sin\beta
=
2
\sin
\left(
\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\right)
\cos
\left(
\frac{\alpha-\beta}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sum-to-product-sine-difference">
<title>Sum-to-Product: Sine Difference</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\alpha-\sin\beta
=
2
\cos
\left(
\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\right)
\sin
\left(
\frac{\alpha-\beta}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sum-to-product-cosine-sum">
<title>Sum-to-Product: Cosine Sum</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\alpha+\cos\beta
=
2
\cos
\left(
\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\right)
\cos
\left(
\frac{\alpha-\beta}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sum-to-product-cosine-difference">
<title>Sum-to-Product: Cosine Difference</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\alpha-\cos\beta
=
-2
\sin
\left(
\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\right)
\sin
\left(
\frac{\alpha-\beta}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-product-to-sum">
<title>Worked Example: Product-to-Sum</title>
<example>
<title>Rewrite a Product</title>
<p>Rewrite</p>

<p>
<md>\sin3x\cos2x</md>
</p>

<p>as a sum.</p>

<p>Use</p>

<p>
<md>\sin\alpha\cos\beta
=
\frac12
\left[
\sin(\alpha+\beta)
+
\sin(\alpha-\beta)
\right].</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\sin3x\cos2x
=
\frac12
\left[
\sin5x+\sin x
\right].</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin3x\cos2x
=
\frac12
(\sin5x+\sin x).
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-sum-to-product">
<title>Worked Example: Sum-to-Product</title>
<example>
<title>Rewrite a Sum</title>
<p>Rewrite</p>

<p>
<md>\cos5x+\cos x.</md>
</p>

<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>\cos\alpha+\cos\beta
=
2
\cos
\left(
\frac{\alpha+\beta}{2}
\right)
\cos
\left(
\frac{\alpha-\beta}{2}
\right),</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\cos5x+\cos x
=
2\cos3x\cos2x.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos5x+\cos x
=
2\cos3x\cos2x.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-sine-cos-reference">
<title>Tangent in Terms of Sine and Cosine</title>
<p>A useful simplification strategy is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan\theta
=
\frac{\sin\theta}{\cos\theta}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot\theta
=
\frac{\cos\theta}{\sin\theta}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Many identity-verification problems become easier after converting all
functions to sine and cosine.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sec-csc-sine-cos">
<title>Secant and Cosecant in Terms of Sine and Cosine</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec\theta
=
\frac{1}{\cos\theta},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc\theta
=
\frac{1}{\sin\theta}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus every elementary trigonometric expression can be rewritten using only
sine and cosine.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-secant-factor-identity">
<title>Useful Identity With Tangent and Secant</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\sec^2\theta-\tan^2\theta=1,</md>
</p>

<p>we can factor:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(\sec\theta-\tan\theta)
(\sec\theta+\tan\theta)
=
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore, where defined,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec\theta-\tan\theta
=
\frac{
1
}{
\sec\theta+\tan\theta
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-csc-cot-factor-identity">
<title>Useful Identity With Cosecant and Cotangent</title>
<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(\csc\theta-\cot\theta)
(\csc\theta+\cot\theta)
=
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Hence,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc\theta-\cot\theta
=
\frac{
1
}{
\csc\theta+\cot\theta
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>where defined.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-auxiliary-angle-reference">
<title>Auxiliary-Angle Form</title>
<p>An expression</p>

<p>
<md>a\cos x+b\sin x</md>
</p>

<p>can be rewritten as</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
R\cos(x-\phi),
}</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
R
=
\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Choose <m>\phi</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\phi
=
\frac{a}{R},
\qquad
\sin\phi
=
\frac{b}{R}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-auxiliary-angle-sine-form">
<title>Alternative Auxiliary-Angle Form</title>
<p>Likewise,</p>

<p>
<md>a\sin x+b\cos x</md>
</p>

<p>can be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
R\sin(x+\phi),
}</md>
</p>

<p>with</p>

<p>
<md>R=\sqrt{a^2+b^2}</md>
</p>

<p>and suitable <m>\phi</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-combining-sine-cosine">
<title>Worked Example: Combining Sine and Cosine</title>
<example>
<title>Rewrite <m>3\cos x+4\sin x</m></title>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>R
=
\sqrt{3^2+4^2}
=
5.</md>
</p>

<p>Choose <m>\phi</m> such that</p>

<p>
<md>\cos\phi
=
\frac35,
\qquad
\sin\phi
=
\frac45.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>5\cos(x-\phi)
=
5
\left(
\cos x\cos\phi
+
\sin x\sin\phi
\right).</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>5\cos(x-\phi)
=
3\cos x+4\sin x.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
3\cos x+4\sin x
=
5\cos(x-\phi),
}</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\phi
=
\arctan\left(\frac43\right).
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-max-linear-trig-combination">
<title>Maximum of a Linear Sine-Cosine Combination</title>
<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>a\cos x+b\sin x
=
R\cos(x-\phi),</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>R=\sqrt{a^2+b^2},</md>
</p>

<p>we have</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
-\sqrt{a^2+b^2}
\leq
a\cos x+b\sin x
\leq
\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\max_x
(a\cos x+b\sin x)
=
\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-complex-exponential-trig">
<title>Complex Exponential Identities</title>
<p>Euler's formula is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e^{i\theta}
=
\cos\theta+i\sin\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its conjugate is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e^{-i\theta}
=
\cos\theta-i\sin\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Adding gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\theta
=
\frac{
e^{i\theta}
+
e^{-i\theta}
}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Subtracting gives</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin\theta
=
\frac{
e^{i\theta}
-
e^{-i\theta}
}{
2i
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-de-moivre-trig-reference">
<title>De Moivre's Formula</title>
<p>For integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^n
=
\cos(n\theta)
+
i\sin(n\theta).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e^{in\theta}
=
\cos(n\theta)
+
i\sin(n\theta).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This provides a systematic way to derive multiple-angle identities.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-chebyshev-trig-connection">
<title>Chebyshev Connection</title>
<p>For the Chebyshev polynomial <m>T_n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
T_n(\cos\theta)
=
\cos(n\theta).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus multiple-angle cosine formulas can be expressed polynomially in
<m>\cos\theta</m>.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
T_3(x)
=
4x^3-3x.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-chebyshev-connection">
<title>Sine Multiple-Angle Connection</title>
<p>Using Chebyshev polynomials of the second kind,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin((n+1)\theta)
=
\sin\theta\,
U_n(\cos\theta).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This connects trigonometric identities with orthogonal polynomials.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-inverse-sine-reference">
<title>Inverse Sine</title>
<p>The inverse sine function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=\arcsin x
}</md>
</p>

<p>with principal range</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
-\frac{\pi}{2}
\leq
y
\leq
\frac{\pi}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its domain is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
-1\leq x\leq1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-inverse-cosine-reference">
<title>Inverse Cosine</title>
<p>The inverse cosine function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=\arccos x
}</md>
</p>

<p>with principal range</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
0\leq y\leq\pi.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its domain is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
-1\leq x\leq1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-inverse-tangent-reference">
<title>Inverse Tangent</title>
<p>The inverse tangent function is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=\arctan x
}</md>
</p>

<p>with principal range</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
-\frac{\pi}{2}
&lt;
y
&lt;
\frac{\pi}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its domain is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x\in\mathbb{R}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-inverse-trig-compositions">
<title>Inverse Function Compositions</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>x\in[-1,1],</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\arcsin x)
=
x.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Likewise,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\arccos x)
=
x.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For every real <m>x</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\arctan x)
=
x.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-reverse-inverse-trig-compositions">
<title>Reverse Compositions Require Range Restrictions</title>
<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>\arcsin(\sin\theta)</md>
</p>

<p>does not equal <m>\theta</m> for every real <m>\theta</m>.</p>

<p>It equals <m>\theta</m> only when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
-\frac{\pi}{2}
\leq
\theta
\leq
\frac{\pi}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\arccos(\cos\theta)
=
\theta</md>
</p>

<p>only when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
0\leq\theta\leq\pi.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-inverse-trig-triangle-method">
<title>Inverse Trig Triangle Method</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>\theta
=
\arctan x.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\tan\theta=x.</md>
</p>

<p>A right triangle may be chosen with:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{opposite}=x,
\qquad
\text{adjacent}=1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus the hypotenuse is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sqrt{1+x^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\arctan x)
=
\frac{x}{\sqrt{1+x^2}},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\arctan x)
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+x^2}}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-useful-inverse-trig-compositions">
<title>Useful Inverse-Trig Compositions</title>
<p>For appropriate domains,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\arccos x)
=
\sqrt{1-x^2},
}</md>
</p>

<p>because</p>

<p>
<md>\arccos x
\in[0,\pi]</md>
</p>

<p>and sine is nonnegative there.</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\arcsin x)
=
\sqrt{1-x^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-arcsine">
<title>Tangent of Arcsine</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>-1&lt;x&lt;1,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\arcsin x)
=
\frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-arccosine">
<title>Tangent of Arccosine</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>-1&lt;x&lt;1,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\arccos x)
=
\frac{\sqrt{1-x^2}}{x}
}</md>
</p>

<p>where the sign and domain interpretation must agree with the principal range
and with <m>x\neq0</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-arctangent-complement">
<title>Arctangent Complement Identity</title>
<p>For <m>x&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\arctan x
+
\arctan\left(\frac1x\right)
=
\frac{\pi}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For <m>x&lt;0</m>, the principal-value relation changes by a sign.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-inverse-tangent-addition">
<title>Tangent Addition for Inverse Tangents</title>
<p>If</p>

<p>
<md>\alpha=\arctan x,
\qquad
\beta=\arctan y,</md>
</p>

<p>then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\alpha+\beta)
=
\frac{x+y}{1-xy}
}</md>
</p>

<p>where the denominator is nonzero.</p>

<p>Recovering <m>\alpha+\beta</m> from inverse tangent requires quadrant and
principal-value care.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-weierstrass-substitution-reference">
<title>Weierstrass Substitution</title>
<p>Set</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
t
=
\tan\left(\frac{x}{2}\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin x
=
\frac{2t}{1+t^2},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos x
=
\frac{1-t^2}{1+t^2},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
dx
=
\frac{2}{1+t^2}\,dt.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This converts rational expressions in sine and cosine into rational
functions of <m>t</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-unit-circle-rational-parameterization">
<title>Pythagorean Parameterization of the Unit Circle</title>
<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>t
=
\tan\left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right),</md>
</p>

<p>the unit circle can be parameterized by</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x
=
\frac{1-t^2}{1+t^2},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y
=
\frac{2t}{1+t^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Indeed,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x^2+y^2=1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-phase-shift-amplitude-form">
<title>Phase-Shift Form</title>
<p>A sinusoid</p>

<p>
<md>A\sin(Bx-C)+D</md>
</p>

<p>can be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
\sin
\left[
B
\left(
x-\frac{C}{B}
\right)
\right]
+
D
}</md>
</p>

<p>when <m>B\neq0</m>.</p>

<p>Thus:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
|A|
=
\text{amplitude},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{2\pi}{|B|}
=
\text{period},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{C}{B}
=
\text{phase shift},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=D
=
\text{midline}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-tangent-transformation-reference">
<title>Tangent Transformation Form</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y
=
A\tan(B(x-C))+D,
}</md>
</p>

<p>the period is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{\pi}{|B|}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The center point is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(C,D).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Vertical asymptotes occur when</p>

<p>
<md>B(x-C)
=
\frac{\pi}{2}
+
k\pi.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x
=
C
+
\frac{
\pi/2+k\pi
}{
B
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sec-csc-periods">
<title>Secant and Cosecant Periods</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>y=A\sec(Bx-C)+D</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>y=A\csc(Bx-C)+D,</md>
</p>

<p>the period is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{2\pi}{|B|}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Their vertical asymptotes occur where their reciprocal denominator vanishes.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-identity-verification-strategy">
<title>Identity Verification Strategy</title>
<p>When verifying a trigonometric identity:</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>usually work on only one side at a time;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>convert secant, cosecant, tangent, and cotangent to sine and cosine
          when helpful;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use Pythagorean identities strategically;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>factor before canceling;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>combine fractions using a common denominator;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use conjugates when expressions such as <m>1\pm\sin x</m> occur;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>apply sum, difference, or double-angle identities when patterns
          appear;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>preserve domain restrictions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>stop once the transformed side matches the other side.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-verify-identity">
<title>Worked Example: Verify an Identity</title>
<example>
<title>Verify a Reciprocal Identity</title>
<p>Verify</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{1-\cos^2x}{\sin x}
=
\sin x.</md>
</p>

<p>Start with the left side.</p>

<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>1-\cos^2x
=
\sin^2x,</md>
</p>

<p>we obtain</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\sin^2x}{\sin x}.</md>
</p>

<p>For <m>\sin x\neq0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{\sin^2x}{\sin x}
=
\sin x.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{1-\cos^2x}{\sin x}
=
\sin x
}</md>
</p>

<p>where the original left side is defined.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-verify-secant">
<title>Worked Example: Identity With Secant</title>
<example>
<title>Worked Example</title>
<p>{Simplify <m>\frac{\sec x-\cos x}{\tan x}</m>}</p>

<p>Convert secant and tangent:</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\frac1{\cos x}-\cos x
}{
\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Simplify the numerator:</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\frac{
1-\cos^2x
}{
\cos x
}
}{
\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Use</p>

<p>
<md>1-\cos^2x
=
\sin^2x.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sin^2x/\cos x
}{
\sin x/\cos x
}
=
\sin x.</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{\sec x-\cos x}{\tan x}
=
\sin x
}</md>
</p>

<p>where the original expression is defined.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-worked-trig-conjugate-technique">
<title>Worked Example: Conjugate Technique</title>
<example>
<title>Worked Example</title>
<p>{Simplify <m>\frac{1-\cos x}{\sin x}</m>}</p>

<p>Multiply numerator and denominator by</p>

<p>
<md>1+\cos x.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{1-\cos x}{\sin x}
\cdot
\frac{1+\cos x}{1+\cos x}
=
\frac{
1-\cos^2x
}{
\sin x(1+\cos x)
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Using</p>

<p>
<md>1-\cos^2x
=
\sin^2x,</md>
</p>

<p>we get</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sin^2x
}{
\sin x(1+\cos x)
}
=
\frac{
\sin x
}{
1+\cos x
}.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{1-\cos x}{\sin x}
=
\frac{\sin x}{1+\cos x}
}</md>
</p>

<p>where both original expressions are defined.</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-small-angle-reference">
<title>Small-Angle Identities and Approximations</title>
<p>These are not exact identities, but important approximations.</p>

<p>As</p>

<p>
<md>x\to0,</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin x
\sim
x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan x
\sim
x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1-\cos x
\sim
\frac{x^2}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These require <m>x</m> measured in radians.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-fundamental-trig-limits-reference">
<title>Fundamental Trigonometric Limits</title>
<p>In radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin x}{x}
=
1,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{1-\cos x}{x^2}
=
\frac12.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These connect trigonometric identities with calculus.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-trig-derivative-reference">
<title>Derivative Identities</title>
<p>The standard derivatives are</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{d}{dx}\sin x
=
\cos x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{d}{dx}\cos x
=
-\sin x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{d}{dx}\tan x
=
\sec^2x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{d}{dx}\cot x
=
-\csc^2x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{d}{dx}\sec x
=
\sec x\tan x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{d}{dx}\csc x
=
-\csc x\cot x.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-trig-integral-reference">
<title>Integral Connections</title>
<p>Important antiderivatives include</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int\sin x\,dx
=
-\cos x+C,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int\cos x\,dx
=
\sin x+C,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int\sec^2x\,dx
=
\tan x+C,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int\csc^2x\,dx
=
-\cot x+C.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-power-reduction-integration">
<title>Power-Reduction in Integration</title>
<p>For integrals involving even powers,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^2x
=
\frac{1-\cos2x}{2},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos^2x
=
\frac{1+\cos2x}{2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>are especially useful.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\int\sin^2x\,dx
=
\frac12
\int
(1-\cos2x)\,dx.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-product-to-sum-integration">
<title>Product-to-Sum in Integration</title>
<p>Products such as</p>

<p>
<md>\sin(mx)\cos(nx)</md>
</p>

<p>can be converted into sums:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(mx)\cos(nx)
=
\frac12
\left[
\sin((m+n)x)
+
\sin((m-n)x)
\right].
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is useful in Fourier analysis and integration.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-sine-cosine-orthogonality">
<title>Orthogonality of Sine and Cosine</title>
<p>For positive integers <m>m,n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
\sin(mx)\sin(nx)\,dx
=
0
\quad
\text{if }m\neq n,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
\cos(mx)\cos(nx)\,dx
=
0
\quad
\text{if }m\neq n.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
\sin(mx)\cos(nx)\,dx
=
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These identities are foundational in Fourier series.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-fourier-basis-norms">
<title>Norms of Fourier Basis Functions</title>
<p>For positive integer <m>n</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
\sin^2(nx)\,dx
=
\pi,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
\cos^2(nx)\,dx
=
\pi.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}1\,dx
=
2\pi.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-harmonic-addition-reference">
<title>Harmonic Addition</title>
<p>Two sinusoidal signals with the same frequency can be combined into a single
sinusoid.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>a\cos(\omega t)
+
b\sin(\omega t)
=
R
\cos(\omega t-\phi),</md>
</p>

<p>where</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
R
=
\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This is important in oscillation and signal models.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-trig-identities-differential-equations">
<title>Trigonometric Identities in Differential Equations</title>
<p>Solutions of the harmonic oscillator</p>

<p>
<md>y''+\omega^2y=0</md>
</p>

<p>can be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y
=
A\cos(\omega t)
+
B\sin(\omega t).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Using auxiliary-angle form,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y
=
R\cos(\omega t-\phi).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus two integration constants can be reinterpreted as amplitude and phase.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-trig-identities-complex-analysis">
<title>Trigonometric Identities in Complex Analysis</title>
<p>Euler's formula gives</p>

<p>
<md>e^{iz}
=
\cos z+i\sin z</md>
</p>

<p>for complex <m>z</m>.</p>

<p>Thus one may define</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos z
=
\frac{
e^{iz}+e^{-iz}
}{2},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin z
=
\frac{
e^{iz}-e^{-iz}
}{
2i
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These extend trigonometric functions to the complex plane.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-trig-hyperbolic-connections">
<title>Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Connections</title>
<p>For real <m>x</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(ix)
=
\cosh x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(ix)
=
i\sinh x.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cosh x
=
\cos(ix),
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sinh x
=
-i\sin(ix).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-trigonometric-identities-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-2x-with-x-2">
<title>Confusing <m>\sin^2x</m> With <m>\sin(x^2)</m></title>
<p>The notation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^2x
}</md>
</p>

<p>means</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(\sin x)^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>It does not mean</p>

<p>
<md>\sin(x^2).</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-1-x-with-reciprocal">
<title>Confusing <m>\sin^{-1}x</m> With Reciprocal</title>
<p>In inverse-function notation,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^{-1}x
=
\arcsin x.
}</md>
</p>

<p>But reciprocal sine is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc x
=
\frac1{\sin x}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>These are different.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsec-false-sine-addition">
<title>Incorrect Sine Addition</title>
<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\alpha+\beta)
\neq
\sin\alpha+\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The correct identity is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\alpha+\beta)
=
\sin\alpha\cos\beta
+
\cos\alpha\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-incorrect-cosine-addition">
<title>Incorrect Cosine Addition</title>
<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha+\beta)
\neq
\cos\alpha+\cos\beta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The correct formula is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha+\beta)
=
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
-
\sin\alpha\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-the-sign-in-cosine-sum-and-difference">
<title>Forgetting the Sign in Cosine Sum and Difference</title>
<p>Remember:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha+\beta)
=
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
-
\sin\alpha\sin\beta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha-\beta)
=
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
+
\sin\alpha\sin\beta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The sign inside the angle is reversed in the middle term.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-the-factor-of-two">
<title>Forgetting the Factor of Two</title>
<p>The sine double-angle identity is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin2x
=
2\sin x\cos x,
}</md>
</p>

<p>not simply</p>

<p>
<md>\sin x\cos x.</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-incorrect-square-root-from-half-angle">
<title>Incorrect Square Root From Half-Angle</title>
<p>From</p>

<p>
<md>\sin^2
\left(
\frac{x}{2}
\right)
=
\frac{1-\cos x}{2},</md>
</p>

<p>we get</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin
\left(
\frac{x}{2}
\right)
=
\pm
\sqrt{
\frac{1-\cos x}{2}
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The sign depends on the quadrant.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-ignoring-trigonometric-domains">
<title>Ignoring Trigonometric Domains</title>
<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\tan x</md>
</p>

<p>is undefined when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos x=0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\sec x</md>
</p>

<p>is undefined at the same points.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-canceling-terms-instead-of-factors">
<title>Canceling Terms Instead of Factors</title>
<p>In general,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{
\sin x+\cos x
}{
\sin x
}</md>
</p>

<p>does not simplify by canceling the <m>\sin x</m> term.</p>

<p>Instead,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{
\sin x+\cos x
}{
\sin x
}
=
1+\cot x.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-dividing-by-a-trigonometric-function-without-checking-zero">
<title>Dividing by a Trigonometric Function Without Checking Zero</title>
<p>From</p>

<p>
<md>\sin x\,A
=
\sin x\,B,</md>
</p>

<p>one may divide by <m>\sin x</m> only when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin x\neq0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Otherwise possible solutions can be lost.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-degree-based-angles-in-calculus-limits">
<title>Using Degree-Based Angles in Calculus Limits</title>
<p>The fundamental limit</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sin x}{x}
=
1
}</md>
</p>

<p>requires radians.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-inverse-trig-composition-always-cancels">
<title>Assuming Inverse-Trig Composition Always Cancels</title>
<p>The identity</p>

<p>
<md>\arcsin(\sin x)=x</md>
</p>

<p>does not hold for every real <m>x</m>.</p>

<p>Principal-value ranges must be respected.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-ignoring-quadrants-in-inverse-tangent-sums">
<title>Ignoring Quadrants in Inverse Tangent Sums</title>
<p>Even if</p>

<p>
<md>\tan\theta
=
z,</md>
</p>

<p>the equation</p>

<p>
<md>\theta=\arctan z</md>
</p>

<p>determines only the principal value.</p>

<p>Additional multiples of <m>\pi</m> may be needed.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-identity-verification-with-equation-solving">
<title>Confusing Identity Verification With Equation Solving</title>
<p>When verifying an identity, the goal is to show two expressions agree on
their common domain.</p>

<p>When solving an equation, the goal is to find specific values of the
variable.</p>

<p>These are different tasks.</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-reciprocal-quotient-trig">
<title>Quick Reciprocal and Quotient Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\csc x
=
\frac1{\sin x}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sec x
=
\frac1{\cos x}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot x
=
\frac1{\tan x}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan x
=
\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cot x
=
\frac{\cos x}{\sin x}
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-pythagorean-trig">
<title>Quick Pythagorean Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^2x+\cos^2x=1
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1+\tan^2x=\sec^2x
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1+\cot^2x=\csc^2x
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-sum-difference-trig">
<title>Quick Sum and Difference Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\alpha+\beta)
=
\sin\alpha\cos\beta
+
\cos\alpha\sin\beta
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin(\alpha-\beta)
=
\sin\alpha\cos\beta
-
\cos\alpha\sin\beta
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha+\beta)
=
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
-
\sin\alpha\sin\beta
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos(\alpha-\beta)
=
\cos\alpha\cos\beta
+
\sin\alpha\sin\beta
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\alpha+\beta)
=
\frac{
\tan\alpha+\tan\beta
}{
1-\tan\alpha\tan\beta
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan(\alpha-\beta)
=
\frac{
\tan\alpha-\tan\beta
}{
1+\tan\alpha\tan\beta
}
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-double-angle-trig">
<title>Quick Double-Angle Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin2x
=
2\sin x\cos x
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos2x
=
\cos^2x-\sin^2x
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos2x
=
2\cos^2x-1
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos2x
=
1-2\sin^2x
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan2x
=
\frac{
2\tan x
}{
1-\tan^2x
}
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-half-angle-trig">
<title>Quick Half-Angle Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin^2\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)
=
\frac{1-\cos x}{2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos^2\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)
=
\frac{1+\cos x}{2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\tan\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)
=
\frac{\sin x}{1+\cos x}
=
\frac{1-\cos x}{\sin x}
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-product-to-sum-trig">
<title>Quick Product-to-Sum Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin A\sin B
=
\frac12
[
\cos(A-B)-\cos(A+B)
]
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos A\cos B
=
\frac12
[
\cos(A-B)+\cos(A+B)
]
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin A\cos B
=
\frac12
[
\sin(A+B)+\sin(A-B)
]
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos A\sin B
=
\frac12
[
\sin(A+B)-\sin(A-B)
]
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-sum-to-product-trig">
<title>Quick Sum-to-Product Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin A+\sin B
=
2
\sin\left(\frac{A+B}{2}\right)
\cos\left(\frac{A-B}{2}\right)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sin A-\sin B
=
2
\cos\left(\frac{A+B}{2}\right)
\sin\left(\frac{A-B}{2}\right)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos A+\cos B
=
2
\cos\left(\frac{A+B}{2}\right)
\cos\left(\frac{A-B}{2}\right)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos A-\cos B
=
-2
\sin\left(\frac{A+B}{2}\right)
\sin\left(\frac{A-B}{2}\right)
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-07-trigonometric-identities-subsec-quick-special-angle-table">
<title>Quick Special-Angle Table</title>
<p>\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c}
<em>Angle</em>
&amp;
<m>\sin\theta</m>
&amp;
<m>\cos\theta</m>
&amp;
<m>\tan\theta</m>
\\ \hline</p>

<p><m>0</m>
&amp;
<m>0</m>
&amp;
<m>1</m>
&amp;
<m>0</m>
\\</p>

<p><m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m>
&amp;
<m>\frac12</m>
&amp;
<m>\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>
&amp;
<m>\frac{\sqrt3}{3}</m>
\\</p>

<p><m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m>
&amp;
<m>\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m>
&amp;
<m>\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m>
&amp;
<m>1</m>
\\</p>

<p><m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m>
&amp;
<m>\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>
&amp;
<m>\frac12</m>
&amp;
<m>\sqrt3</m>
\\</p>

<p><m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>
&amp;
<m>1</m>
&amp;
<m>0</m>
&amp;
undefined
\end{tabular}</p>
</subsection>

